WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.465
This video is about detective work: decoding a kernel oops line by line, then tracing and profiling with ftrace and perf.

00:00:07.865 --> 00:00:14.587
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:19.632
Kernel oops: The kernel's crash report: which function failed, at which address, with the call stack. Like: A black-box flight recorder printout.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:26.198
ftrace / perf: Built-in Linux tracing (ftrace) and profiling (perf) tools that show where time goes. Like: A stopwatch and a step counter clipped onto the kernel.

00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:32.368
Overnight, a bar in a customer's office reboots, and the log shows a wall of hexadecimal.

00:00:32.618 --> 00:00:35.869
Most engineers scroll past it. You'll read it like a map.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:39.881
Let's decode a kernel crash and meet the tools that find the cause.

00:00:40.931 --> 00:00:42.823
Debugging is detective work.

00:00:43.223 --> 00:00:45.371
The kernel log is the witness statement.

00:00:45.771 --> 00:00:49.788
An oops is the chalk outline. It shows exactly where it happened.

00:00:50.188 --> 00:00:52.672
Ftrace is the CCTV timeline.

00:00:53.072 --> 00:00:56.184
Perf is the traffic counter: where the time goes.

00:00:56.584 --> 00:01:00.148
And JTAG freezes time, so you can walk around the scene.

00:01:00.548 --> 00:01:09.441
JTAG / SWD: Hardware debug ports that let a debugger stop the processor and read its memory. Like: A doctor's stethoscope plugged straight into the chip.

00:01:10.541 --> 00:01:11.238
Words.

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:17.907
An oops is the kernel's error report, with registers and a call trace. It may or may not crash the system.

00:01:18.307 --> 00:01:24.670
P C is the program counter, where it failed. L R is the link register, where it would return to.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:30.004
Tainted flags tell you about the kernel's state. O means an out of tree module was loaded.

00:01:30.404 --> 00:01:34.212
F addr two line maps a function plus offset to a source line.

00:01:34.612 --> 00:01:37.038
And ftrace is the kernel's built in tracer.

00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:41.396
Let's decode lab twelve's oops, line by line.

00:01:41.796 --> 00:01:50.864
Line one: a null pointer dereference at address hex eighteen. That means code read a field at offset twenty four bytes inside a struct, through a null pointer.

00:01:51.264 --> 00:01:57.603
Line two: it's an oops, number one. The first oops is the one that matters; later ones are often fallout.

00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:01.683
The modules list shows our out of tree bridge driver was loaded.

00:02:02.083 --> 00:02:06.704
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:02:06.954 --> 00:02:12.747
It happened on CPU two, in a kernel worker thread, and the kernel is tainted by an out of tree module.

00:02:13.147 --> 00:02:17.269
The workqueue line names the work function: polybridge h p d work.

00:02:17.669 --> 00:02:27.886
Workqueue / bottom half: A kernel queue that runs deferred work later in a normal context where sleeping is allowed. Like: Putting the parcel on the table to open properly after you have closed the door.

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:41.142
EDID / HPD: EDID is the screen's description of what it can show; hot-plug detect (HPD) is the wire that says a screen is connected. Like: A guest's dietary card (EDID) and the doorbell that says they arrived (HPD).

00:02:41.442 --> 00:02:45.378
P C gives the exact place: sixty bytes into that function.

00:02:45.778 --> 00:02:51.154
The call trace shows it came straight from the workqueue machinery, not from probe or an interrupt.

00:02:51.554 --> 00:02:57.254
probe(): The driver function the kernel calls when it finds a device that matches the driver. Like: The first handshake when a translator is introduced to the person they will translate for.

00:02:57.504 --> 00:03:07.616
IRQ / interrupt: Interrupt request: a hardware signal that makes the processor stop and handle an event right now. Like: A doorbell: you stop what you are doing and answer it.

00:03:07.916 --> 00:03:20.060
Now map it to a source line with f addr two line. In this scenario, the work ran after remove had freed the bridge state. The fix: cancel work sync in remove, and only schedule work after probe completes.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.000
Tracing recipes. Go to the tracing directory.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.475
Choose the function graph tracer, which shows call trees with timings.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:32.567
Limit it to d w c three functions, and turn tracing on.

00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:46.191
DWC3 (Synopsys IP): A ready-made USB controller design that Synopsys sells and many Qualcomm and NXP chips include. Like: A front desk bought from a furniture catalogue: many hotels have the same model, so one driver fits them all.

00:03:46.491 --> 00:03:50.253
Or enable d w c three's trace events, and stream them.

00:03:50.653 --> 00:03:53.602
Trace c m d wraps all of this in one command.

00:03:54.002 --> 00:04:00.712
Perf record with call graphs, across all CPUs, finds CPU hogs, like a memcpy in a video path.

00:04:01.112 --> 00:04:06.523
And dynamic debug turns on a module's debug prints at run time, without rebuilding.

00:04:07.723 --> 00:04:08.779
More tools.

00:04:09.179 --> 00:04:13.428
Debug builds with memory, race and lock checkers, and a leak detector.

00:04:13.828 --> 00:04:19.064
P store and ram oops keep logs across a reboot, so you can read a crash that reset the board.

00:04:19.464 --> 00:04:23.296
JTAG for hangs before the console, and for multi core lockups.

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:30.104
On Android: tombstones for native crashes, bug reports, simple perf, and Perfetto traces.

00:04:30.504 --> 00:04:35.578
And git bisect run, with an automated test, finds the commit that broke something.

00:04:36.778 --> 00:04:44.986
Question for you: A crash log says the program counter was at polybridge_hpd_work plus 0x3c. What do you do with that?

00:04:50.286 --> 00:04:58.657
Answer: Use the matching build's symbols, for example with faddr2line, to turn that offset into the exact source file and line.

00:04:59.757 --> 00:05:00.454
Recap.

00:05:00.854 --> 00:05:03.803
An oops tells you what, where, and how.

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:07.105
The fault address offset points to the struct field.

00:05:07.505 --> 00:05:09.955
F addr two line gives the source line.

00:05:10.355 --> 00:05:13.269
Ftrace for timelines, perf for CPU time.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:16.850
And every fix gets a regression test and a short write up.
